<p>22. <strong>Did you work with agile methodologies</strong>? (Mitza) <ac:emoticon ac:name="tick" /><br /><strong>[Scrum]</strong> Yes, I worked with Scrum using sprints of 2 weeks, we had planning ceremony at the beginning of the sprint, daily stand up meetings to give our daily status, check the priorities and report any blockers we could have, and at the end of the sprint we do a demo to the stakeholders about the  progress made during the sprint and a retrospective ceremony to detect points of improvements.<br /><strong>[Kanban]</strong> I worked with Kanban too using a prioritized backlog and taking the task at the top of the backlog with the highest priority. This methodology is very good when the work is ‘on demand’ and the priorities change often causing that it’s not possible to plan the work to include in a sprint.<br /><strong>[Scrumban]</strong> Also, I worked with Scrumban that it’s a mix of these 2 adapted to the client necessities, in our case, we used the prioritized backlog from Kanban and the sprints from Scrum to review the progress made by the team and make retrospectives.<em style="letter-spacing: 0.0px;"><br /><br /></em></p>
<p>23. <strong>How should QA work in agile environments</strong>? (Mitza) <ac:emoticon ac:name="tick" /><br />Once the requirements are available, QA team should start to analyze them and create the scenarios to be tested, in this way, when the development is ready to be tested, the scenarios are also ready to be executed by the testers. It’s important to be aligned with the priorities and the development plan to produce the scenarios that will be required first, and stay aware about any change in the requirements to update the scenarios written and create new scenarios if it’s necessary.<em style="letter-spacing: 0.0px;"><br /><br /></em></p>
<p>24. <strong>What mistakes did you make? Why? How did you solve them?</strong> (Mitza) <ac:emoticon ac:name="tick" /><br />At the beginning of my career I reported as bugs some changes in the requirements because I wasn’t aware of theses changes. <br />Other mistake I did it was don’t agree the way to report the incidents to the team, so the team wasn’t aware about these issues. <br />Those two mistake were solved improving the communication with the team and defining processes and channels of communication to keep everybody in the same page.<em style="letter-spacing: 0.0px;"><br /><br /></em></p>
