<p>31.<strong> What do you do when you don't have documentation? </strong>(Tony) <ac:emoticon ac:name="tick" /><strong><br /></strong>It depends. If I don't have anything (documentation, a user story, or any card in the backlog used by the company) I DM or set up a meeting with the dev that has worked that and start making questions on what he/she has done, and why work in that way. In my experience when you do this a couple of questions are raised that make the dev notice that maybe something was missing and I start working focusing on that.<br />I also send to the BA or Product Owner or any other point of contact a very small, but very focused set of behaviors writting in BDD to make sure that I'm understanding the expected behavior correctly, and if I have the time create at least the test cases.<br /><br />In case that we dont have anyone to ask the questions, we will do exploratory testing and document the scenarios covered.<br /><br /></p>
<p>32.<strong> Have you ever done production support? </strong>(Tony) (This is a personal answer) <ac:emoticon ac:name="tick" /><strong><br /></strong>Yes. I have worked monitoring production servers using differents tools like Grafana and logging tools like Kibana to create searchs and views that allows to check if the numbers of errors or the use on the app has increase, decrease or deviate. I also have created alerts that triggers when a server stop logging.<br /><br /></p>
<p>33.<strong> Have you tried applications with a web interface? </strong>(Tony) <ac:emoticon ac:name="tick" /><strong><br /></strong>Yes. My main knowledge is on API testing, but I offen do some UI testing. When I do this I make sure to check different resolutions and browser to make sure that everithing is working fine no matter what monitor or device the user is using.<br /><br /></p>
<p>34. <strong>How do you defend your position in a reported defect when they want to change its severity?</strong> (Mitza) <ac:image ac:alt="(tick)"><ri:url ri:value="https://confluence.endava.com/s/-8ae3xu/9012/tu5x00/_/images/icons/emoticons/check.svg" /></ac:image><br />First of all, I'll make sure they are not confusing priority with severity by explaining that severity is the impact of the defect in the application, meanwhile priority is about how important is fix the defect. If after the clarification I confirm they want to change the severity, I'll ask them about their fundaments and I'll expouse mine to finally find an agreement.<br /><br /></p>
<p>35. <strong>What do you know about the principles of testing?</strong> (Reference: ISTQB Foundation) (Mitza) <ac:image ac:alt="(tick)"><ri:url ri:value="https://confluence.endava.com/s/-8ae3xu/9012/tu5x00/_/images/icons/emoticons/check.svg" /></ac:image><br />The 7 principles of testing are:</p>
<ol>
<li style="list-style-type: none;">
<ol>
<li class="TrT0Xe" style="list-style-type: disc;"><em>Testing shows the presence of defects</em>: Even if defects are not found, this doesn't mean they don't exists.</li>
<li class="TrT0Xe" style="list-style-type: disc;"><em>Exhaustive testing is impossible</em>: Determine the optimal amount of testing based on the risk assessment.</li>
<li class="TrT0Xe" style="list-style-type: disc;"><em>Early testing</em>: Testing should start as early as possible, when it's cheaper fix software and design.</li>
<li class="TrT0Xe" style="list-style-type: disc;"><em>Defect clustering</em>: 80% of the problems are found in the 20% of the modules.</li>
<li class="TrT0Xe" style="list-style-type: disc;"><em>Pesticide paradox</em>: Repeat the same set of tests become useless to discover new defects.</li>
<li class="TrT0Xe" style="list-style-type: disc;"><em>Testing is context dependent</em>: Different systems are tested in different ways. Testing methodologies and techniques are choose based on the system to test.</li>
<li class="TrT0Xe" style="list-style-type: disc;"><em style="letter-spacing: 0.0px;">Absence-of-errors fallacy</em><span style="letter-spacing: 0.0px;">: A system 99% bugs-free still can be unusable if it doesn't fulfill the user's need and requirements.</span></li></ol></li></ol>
<p><br />36. <strong>What are positive and negative tests?</strong> (Mitza) <ac:image ac:alt="(tick)"><ri:url ri:value="https://confluence.endava.com/s/-8ae3xu/9012/tu5x00/_/images/icons/emoticons/check.svg" /></ac:image><br />Positive tests are those which validated that the application is working correctly in the expected conditions, in the meanwhile, the negative tests are those which validated the ability of the application to response to the unexpected or wrong conditions.<br /><br /></p>
<p>37. <strong>What is the difference between priority and severity?</strong> (Mika)</p>
<p class="p1">Defect Severity means how the defect has affected the application’s functionality. There are levels of severity as Critical, High, Medium and low.</p>
<ul>
<li class="p1">Critical: A defect has complete blocked the functionality of the application. The user cannot proceed on the functionality.</li>
<li class="p1">High: comparing with the previously (Critical) in this case the defect does not affect the whole application but affect significant system functionalities. It is still risky for user experiencie and business.</li>
<li class="p1">Medium: The behavior of an application is different than expected, but this does not affect functionality in a risky way.</li>
<li class="p1">Low: Defect has a minor affect product operation. It could be a domestic nature o look and feel defects that don’t affect the application functionality but they are valid defect. The fixed could improve the good experience when user is navigating in the application (UX)</li></ul>
<p class="p1">Defect Priority means how fast defect should be fixed and it depends on the priority and the business perspective of the project. For example a low defect related to spelling mistake could has high priority if it related to law agreement.</p>
<ul>
<li class="p1">High: These bugs may affect the whole application. They need resolution as soon as possible, and assigning a high priority ensures low resolution time. Usually, a high severity means a high priority as well, but it depends on the business rule as we see in the example above.</li>
<li class="p1">Medium: The defects which don’t affect business and customers typically get Medium priority. They are less urgent than high-priority defects and can be fixed when the development team has the bandwidth to take them up. </li>
<li class="p1">Low: As they have a minor affect product operation, they could be fixed after mediums defects. They don’t have a significant impact if they are not resolve immediately but they are still defects. </li></ul>
<p>38. <strong>What methodologies do you know?</strong> (Mika)</p>
<p class="p1">The software testing methodologies are the strategies, processes, or environments used for testing in SDLC (Software Development Life Cycle). The most common types are:</p>
<ul>
<li class="p1"><strong>Waterfall Model:</strong> Formal testing starts once the development phase is completed. The waterfall testing model works well for small and less complex projects. However, if the requirements are not clearly defined upfront, it is extremely difficult to go back and make changes to completed phases. The errors that are found later in development are costly to fix.</li>
<li class="p1"><strong>Agile Model:</strong> It is most suitable for larger development projects. Agile testing is an incremental model where testing is done at the end of each increment or iteration. Additionally, the entire application is tested at the end of the project. There is less risk in the development process with the Agile model because each team member understands what has been completed or not. Project development outcomes are usually better with Agile when there is a strong and experienced project manager who can make quick decisions.</li>
<li class="p1"><strong>DevOps Approach and Continuous Testing: </strong>By taking a DevOps approach to testing, or continuous testing, there is collaboration with operations teams throughout the entire product lifecycle. Through this collaboration, development and operations teams do not wait until the software is built or nearly finished to conduct testing. This means the software delivery process is faster, defects are detected earlier, and their resolution is less costly. Continuous testing uses automated testing and automation tools as components of the software development pipeline to provide immediate feedback on any business risks that may exist.</li></ul>
