<li><strong>What is a RESTFul Web Service?</strong><br />
<p><em>RESTful web services are web services that adhere to the principles of the REST architectural style. They use HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, etc.) to manipulate resources, and they use URIs (Uniform Resource Identifiers) to identify and access those resources.</em></p>
<p><em>In a RESTful web service, resources are represented as documents or data structures, and they can be accessed and manipulated using a standard set of HTTP methods. For example, a RESTful web service might use the GET method to retrieve a resource, the POST method to create a new resource, the PUT method to update an existing resource, and the DELETE method to remove a resource.</em></p>
<p><em>In REST, resources are manipulated using HTTP methods such as GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE. Each HTTP method corresponds to a specific action that can be performed on a resource:</em></p>
<p><em>GET: Retrieves the current state of a resource.</em></p>
<p><em>POST: Creates a new resource.</em></p>
<p><em>PUT: Updates an existing resource or creates a new resource if it does not exist.</em></p>
<p><em>DELETE: Removes a resource.</em></p>
<p><em>By using resources as the basis for web services and APIs, REST enables a clear separation between the client and server components of an application, as well as a consistent and standardized interface for accessing and manipulating those resources.<br /><br /></em></p></li>
