<li><strong>What is a test framework? (Paulina Luna)</strong>
<p><em>A test framework is a set of guidelines, tools, and practices that help structure and manage automated tests. It includes features for running test cases, reporting results, managing test data, and organizing the code efficiently.</em></p>
<p><em>For example, in my case, I use JUnit as the test framework with Java, which helps structure the tests, set up test conditions, and report results.<br /><br /></em></p></li>
<li><strong>What are the apparent advantages of a test framework? (Paulina Luna)</strong>
<p><em>Using a test framework brings a lot of advantages. It helps keep the code organized and reusable, especially when using something like the Page Object Model design pattern. It also makes test scripts easier to read and maintain, which is really helpful as the project grows.</em></p>
<p><em>Most frameworks also come with built-in reporting and logging, so it’s easier to track test results and spot issues. They usually integrate well with CI/CD tools, which helps with automation in the development pipeline. And when it comes to scaling, frameworks let you run tests in parallel and manage large test suites more efficiently. Overall, a good framework brings structure, consistency, and reliability to the testing process.<br /><br /></em></p></li>
<li>Have you built frameworks from scratch using selenium web driver or have you used ready-made frameworks?</li>
<li><strong>What are the attributes of a good framework? (Paulina Luna) <br /></strong><br /><em>A good automation framework should be easy to maintain and reuse, especially as the app grows. It should support scalability, like running tests in parallel, and make tests clear and readable. Being able to configure environments easily, integrate with tools like Git and CI/CD, and generate useful reports is also important. And if it supports data-driven testing, it helps cover more scenarios efficiently without duplicating code.<br /><br /></em></li>
<li>How do you order your tests? (In the code)</li>
<li><strong>Do you know what Page Object Model (POM) is? (Paulina Luna)</strong><br />
<p><em>Yes, Page Object Model is a design pattern where each page of the application is represented by a class with locators and methods. It helps keep test code clean, reusable, and easy to maintain.<br /><br /></em></p></li>
<li><strong>When is it preferable to use the Page Object Model? and when not? (Paulina Luna)</strong><br />
<p><em>I think Page Object Model is really useful in medium to large projects, especially when there’s a lot of repeated interactions with the same pages. It makes the code easier to maintain and understand.</em></p>
<p><em>But in very small projects or quick exploratory tests, using POM can be a bit too much. So, I try to keep things simple when it makes sense. If I know the project will grow or be reused later, then I definitely go with POM.<br /><br /></em></p></li>
<li><strong>When should it be automated? (Paulina Luna)</strong><br />
<p><em>A test should be automated when it's stable, repetitive, and run frequently — like regression or smoke tests. It’s also worth automating when fast feedback is needed or when testing across multiple data sets or browsers. But if the test is exploratory or the feature is still changing a lot, manual testing is usually better.<br /><br /></em></p></li>
<li><strong>Things on a page you wouldn't automate? (Paulina Luna)</strong><br />
<p><em>I usually avoid automating things that are unstable or change frequently, like animations, hover effects, or content that updates dynamically without much user impact. I also skip purely visual elements like layout alignment or font styles — those are better suited for visual testing tools or manual review. Exploratory areas or early-stage features still in design also aren’t ideal for automation.<br /><br /></em></p></li>
<li><strong>Do you know the concept of BDD? (Paulina Luna)</strong><br />
<p><em>Yes, BDD stands for Behavior-Driven Development. It’s a collaborative approach where developers, testers, and business stakeholders define application behavior using clear, human-readable language.</em></p>
<p><em>In testing, BDD is usually implemented using tools like Cucumber, where test scenarios are written in Gherkin syntax — using 'Given, When, Then' steps. This makes tests easy to understand for both technical and non-technical team members and helps ensure that everyone is aligned on the expected behavior of a feature.<br /><br /></em></p></li>
<li>List the advantages of using Selenium as a testing tool? What about Cypress? Advantages and disadvantages</li>
<li><strong>Naming Element Selectors in Automation. (Paulina Luna)</strong><br />
<p><em><span>I name element selectors based on their function, like </span><span>loginButton</span><span> or </span><span>emailInput</span><span>, to keep the code readable and meaningful. I follow consistent naming conventions, especially when using Page Object Model, and avoid generic names like 'button1' or 'input2'.</span></em></p></li>
